    "result_text": "The full test suite is green. `AcceptanceTest` is the only concrete test class (the other files are a base class and a helper), and both its tests pass with `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented telephone normalization on owner creation (`POST /api/owners`), which flows through the OfficeFloor pipeline `RequireOwnerFields \u2192 BuildOwner \u2192 SaveOwner \u2192 RespondWithOwnerCreated`.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`RequireOwnerFields.java`** (the pipeline's first/validation step) \u2014 after the existing required-field check, it now strips every non-digit character from the telephone (`replaceAll(\"\\\\D\", \"\")`), requires exactly 10 digits, and writes the normalized value back onto the request DTO before publishing it. Because the DTO is mutated in place, the stored `Owner` entity and the `GET` response both return the 10-digit value.\n\n2. **`InvalidTelephoneException.java`** (new checked exception) + **`InvalidTelephoneExceptionHandler.java`** (new handler) \u2014 thrown when the stripped telephone isn't exactly 10 digits; the handler responds **400** with an RFC-7807 `ProblemDetail`, mirroring the existing `MissingFieldsException` handling.\n\n3. **`officefloor/escalation/\u2026InvalidTelephoneException.yml`** (new) \u2014 wires the exception to its handler, per the project's escalation convention rather than `@ControllerAdvice`.\n\n**Behavior now matches the spec:**\n- `\"(04) 1234-5678\"` \u2192 normalized and stored as `\"0412345678\"` (verified by `coreStripsToTenDigits`).\n- `\"12345\"` \u2192 400 (verified by `errorRejectsTooFewDigits`).\n\nI kept normalization in the existing validation step rather than adding a separate function class, since that step already binds `@RequestBody` (only one step per pipeline may) and publishes the DTO for downstream steps.",
